A||||12||Willy||Headlight problem
Z||000000||Willy||07-09-99||01:13 AM||||I just got home from work and went to shut my lights off on my '86 GT and both lights went down.  But the right headlight motor after going down kept on turning and clicked continously until I turned the lights back on and both went up.  Finaly had to disconnect motor so I could turn the lights off.
Z||000001||Cliff Pennock||07-09-99||02:25 AM||||There are <b>three</b> relais that control the headlight motors. Swap the two that are nearest to the headlights (each headlight has a small black box with - I think - three wires going out of them, those are the relais). If the problem moves from the right to the left headlight motor then simply replace the broken relais.
Z||000002||Carrolles||07-09-99||06:55 AM||||It sounds more like dirty limit switch contact points to me if it is an 86.  These can be found under a small rectangular cover plate (about 1" x 2") on the side of the actuator motor.  It is easiest to work on this if you remove the motor from the car and take it to a work bench.  Once you remove the three screws and cover plate you will see the limit switch assembly.  It slides out.  Be careful not to let anything else fall out when you remove the limit switch.  You will see that it has two sets of contact points much like an old set of distributor points.  Try cleaning them with fine emory cloth or sand paper.<br>I believe if it was a bad relay the light would not go down (or up) at all.<br>Hope this helps,<br>Carroll
Z||000003||Cliff Pennock||07-09-99||10:09 AM||||I thought the master relay supplies power to both the headlight motors and the two seperate relays just makes the motors stop whenever they are fully opened/closed. Then again, I could be completely wrong. Darn. Now I'll have to look it up in the Haynes manual again... ;)<br>
Z||000004||theogre||07-09-99||10:05 PM||||if the motor keeps running, the gear could be on it's way out. Gears can be a bit more worn on one face than the other, and that's all it takes to have a problem.<p>If you are going thru the trouble of checking the limiters, check the gears too. At the least you can stuff some grease in them.<p>Being that both motors started at the exact same time, you also need to check the relays. All of them, how ever many that is. if there is any question at all, replace all of them. Relays are a hell of alot cheaper than motors. And considering the relays are over a decade old,,,,,, it's cheap insurance.
Z||000005||PaUL||07-10-99||08:03 AM||||I agree with Carrolles, it MUST be the limit switches which are simple mechanical reed type switches. These are supposed to turn the motor off when fully open or closed. Fieros came with 2 different headlight motor designs. The newer ones dont have the limit switches, using an external electronic controller instead. 
Z||000006||theogre||07-11-99||06:52 PM||||Those new motors started in 87.... I like them very much. The box reads the current draw of the motor and shuts them off at a certain value.... I suspect this helps if the thing is frozen shut in the winter as well, but I'm not testing it on mine. :)<p>in the old motors, the only way the motor can keep spinning is if the gears are hosed. A bad/dirty limiter could cause the gears to be trashed, but once they are trashed they are trashed. Cleaning the limiters won't help trashed gears, but it may hide them for awhile. I still think if you are going to fool with the limiters, you should at least check the gears and if nothing else grease them up good.
Z||000007||PaUL||07-12-99||02:13 AM||||yeah it could be the gears also. They are made of a cheap plastic that becomes crumbly with age.
Z||000008||DJRice||07-12-99||10:56 AM||||In the event you do decide that you need to replace a gear, which isnt a big job at all, you can get the parts you need from Ecklers Corvettes (ecklers.com) here in Titusville, FL.  Fortunately, Fiero, Firebird, Vette all used the same motors.  The gear for the 84-86 is about $20 and the bushings that fail in the 87-88 are only about $5.  <p>
Z||000009||Carrolles||07-12-99||11:33 AM||||You can aslo reuse the worn out gear by turning it around.  Usually when they strip out it effects less than 180 degrees of rotation of the gear.  Turn it around to place the unworn teeth in service.
Z||000010||theogre||07-12-99||08:03 PM||||I've heard of ecklers.... Unless they've changed, you have to fool with a file to fir the shearpins they have into Fiero motors. Fiero's are close but not exactly the same.<p>Rodney dickman has drop in pins, and a bunch of other stuff. some of it's pretty nice, and all of it's reasonably priced from what I've seen.
Z||000011||DJRice||07-13-99||08:27 AM||||Ogre, are you talking about buying the entire replacement motors?  All I did was buy the bushing kit for my 87 and my headlights were going up and down again in about an hour (per side).  <p>I actually bought the gear for my 84 too, before I realized that it only turned around halfway.  It was just a straight swap out also.  As long as you dont count the rusted screw that the head snapped off of.  And drill bit I broke trying to get that darn <br>screw out of the hole.  And the tetanus shot I got after I broke the drill bit and the drill slipped and punched a hole in my index finger.  Other than that, It was no problem at all........hehe<p>
Z||000012||theogre||07-13-99||02:05 PM||||No, I didn't mean you neeeded a whole motor for an 87+. With the current limiting controller, and the shear pins inside, these motore should last a heck of a long time.<p>The pre 87 ones can get really scragged. Sometimes you get lucky and you just flip the gear, but not always. Anyone who Is pullling a part a pre 87 one should also check the limiters, if they are scummy or shot you just break a new gear up.
